---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Process from '../components/Process.astro';
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro';
import Reviews from '../components/Reviews.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import QuoteSection from '../components/QuoteSection.astro';
import ServicesGrid from '../components/ServicesGrid.astro';
import { CTA } from '../data/site';
import { CERAMIC, CERAMIC_ADDONS } from '../data/pricing';
import { breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, serviceSchema } from '../data/schema';

const path = '/ceramic-coating/';
const title = 'Ceramic Coating Oakland, CA | Applied at Your Home | J&W';
const description = 'Professional ceramic coating in Oakland, applied at your home. Decontamination and paint correction included. Bronze, Silver and Gold packages, quoted for your vehicle and paint.';

// The questions people actually ask before they buy a coating, answered
// straight. No lifespan promises: the expected life of the specific coating
// is part of the quote, because it depends on the product, the prep and how
// the car is washed.
const faqs = [
  { q: 'What does a ceramic coating actually do?', a: 'It bonds a hard, glass-like layer to your clear coat. Once cured, that layer takes the abuse the paint used to take: sun works on the coating instead of oxidizing the clear coat, bird droppings, tree sap and bug splatter sit on top instead of etching in, brake dust and road film cannot bond, and water beads and sheets off instead of drying into spots and grime. Because it goes on over freshly corrected paint, the finish also looks deeper and glossier, and the coating locks that clarity in. The everyday result is a car that stays cleaner longer, washes in half the time and keeps its colour.' },
  { q: 'Is a ceramic coating worth it?', a: 'For most people who keep a car more than a year and park it outside at least some of the time, yes. The value is not one big moment, it is hundreds of small ones: rinsing off a week of dust in five minutes, sap that wipes away instead of etching, paint that is still deep and unfaded when you sell or trade the car. It is worth the most on new or freshly corrected paint, dark colours, cars that live outdoors, and anyone who dislikes washing. It is worth the least on paint whose clear coat is already failing, which no coating can fix.' },
  { q: 'How long does a ceramic coating last?', a: 'A professional coating is measured in years, not months. How many depends on the grade of coating, how well the paint was prepared, whether the car is garaged, and above all how it is washed. Hand or touchless washing with a pH-neutral soap keeps it working; brush car washes, harsh chemicals and contamination left to bake all shorten it. When we quote your vehicle we tell you the expected life of the specific coating we will use, and exactly how to get all of it.' },
  { q: 'Does a ceramic coating stop scratches and rock chips?', a: 'No. A coating is a few microns thick. It resists the fine swirls that washing puts into paint and it shrugs off chemical damage, but it cannot absorb a rock at freeway speed, a key or a shopping cart. That is what paint protection film (PPF) is for: a thick, clear urethane film that takes the impact. Many owners do both, film on the hood, bumper and mirrors where chips happen, and a coating over the whole car for gloss, chemical resistance and easy cleaning. For a daily driver that is not always behind trucks on the freeway, a coating alone is usually the right call.' },
  { q: 'Do I need paint correction before a ceramic coating?', a: 'Yes. A coating locks in whatever is under it, so swirls, water spots and haze sealed beneath a coating stay there for its entire life. Correction removes them first, and a corrected, fully decontaminated surface is also what lets the coating bond properly to the clear coat. It is built into every package: an enhancement polish with Bronze for paint already in good shape, one-step correction with Silver, and two-step compounding and polishing with Gold for heavily swirled, oxidized or dark paint.' },
  { q: 'Can I coat a brand-new car?', a: 'It is the best time. New paint has the fewest defects to correct, and coating it before its first automatic wash means it never picks up the swirls most cars carry within a year. It still needs decontamination and a light polish, because transport, the dealer lot and the pre-delivery wash leave more behind than you would think. A professionally applied coating does not affect the manufacturer\'s paint warranty. If a dealership offers you a protection package, ask what product is applied and whether any correction is done before you pay for it. Teslas, Rivians and other cars with thin, soft paint benefit the most.' },
  { q: 'How do I wash a ceramic-coated car?', a: 'Gently and regularly. Two-bucket hand wash with a pH-neutral car shampoo and a clean microfiber mitt, every two to three weeks if the car lives outside, then dry it with plush microfiber towels or a blower rather than letting it air-dry. Touchless car washes are fine. Avoid brush and cloth tunnel washes, degreasers, and anything acidic or strongly alkaline on the paint. Do not put wax or a paint sealant over a coating; they cannot bond to it and only mask how it behaves. A maintenance detail once or twice a year keeps it performing.' },
  { q: 'Can I take a coated car through an automatic car wash?', a: 'Touchless, yes. Brush or cloth tunnels, no: the spinning brushes carry grit from every car before yours, and they are the fastest way to put swirls into a coating. If a touchless wash is your routine, dry the car afterwards, because the recycled water those systems use leaves mineral spots.' },
  { q: 'How long does the job take, and when can I drive and wash the car?', a: 'A complete coating with one-step correction is a full-day appointment. Two-step correction can run longer or split over two visits. You can drive the car the same evening. Keep it dry and out of direct rain, sprinklers and bird droppings for the first 24 hours while the coating hardens, and do not wash it at all for the first week; no soap, chemicals or waxes for two weeks. If it gets rained on early, dry it, do not scrub it. You get written cure and care instructions for the exact coating we used.' },
  { q: 'Will a ceramic coating stop water spots?', a: 'It makes them less likely and easier to remove, not impossible. Water spots are minerals left behind when tap or sprinkler water dries on the paint. A coating keeps them from bonding into the clear coat, but they can still etch the coating itself if left in the sun for weeks. Dry the car after washing, keep it away from lawn sprinklers, and wipe fresh spots with a quick detailer as soon as you see them. Caught early they come off; ignored, they need polishing.' },
  { q: 'What can damage a ceramic coating?', a: 'Abrasion and chemistry, in that order. Brush car washes, dirty wash mitts and dry wiping put fine scratches into it. Strong wheel cleaners, degreasers, tar removers and alkaline truck-wash soaps eat into it. Bird droppings, bug splatter and tree sap left to bake in the sun will etch through eventually. Neglect is the quiet one: a coating that is never washed gets buried in road film and stops beading, which people mistake for the coating failing. Regular gentle washing prevents all of it.' },
  { q: 'How do I know the coating is still working?', a: 'Water behaviour is the tell. A working coating beads tightly and sheets off when you rinse. When beading goes flat and the paint stays dusty after a wash, it is almost always road film sitting on top of the coating, not the coating wearing out, and a decontamination wash brings it back. When the paint stops beading even after a proper decontamination, the coating has reached the end of its life and it is time to correct and re-coat.' },
  { q: 'Can wheels, glass, trim, wraps or PPF be coated too?', a: 'Yes, with coatings made for each surface. Wheel coatings shrug off brake dust so wheels rinse clean instead of needing harsh cleaners. Glass coatings sheet rain off the windshield at speed and make bugs and ice easier to clear. Trim coatings keep black plastic dark. Wraps, paint protection film and matte paint can be coated with matte-safe products that protect without adding gloss. We quote these as add-ons to the paint.' },
  { q: 'What is the difference between a spray-on ceramic and a professional coating?', a: 'Spray ceramics and ceramic-infused waxes are toppers: a thin layer applied in an afternoon that lasts a few months, with no prep. A professional coating is a far denser product applied panel by panel over corrected, decontaminated paint, then cured, and it protects for years. Sprays are a good way to maintain a professional coating between details. They are not a substitute for one.' },
  { q: 'How much does a ceramic coating cost?', a: 'We quote every coating for the vehicle in front of us, because two things decide the price: the size of the vehicle, since more panels mean more prep and more product, and how much paint correction goes under the coating, which is what separates Bronze, Silver and Gold. Send us the make and model and a photo of the paint in direct sun. We tell you which package your paint needs and give you a set price before anything is booked. Glass and interior coatings are quoted with it. A quote far below the going rate usually means the correction is being skipped, and correction is most of the work.' },
  { q: 'Do you come to me for ceramic coating? What do you need?', a: 'Yes. We apply coatings at your home or workplace across Oakland, Alameda, Berkeley, San Leandro, Piedmont, Emeryville, Orinda and Walnut Creek. We need a garage, carport or shaded driveway out of direct sun and rain, and about a day. We bring water, power and lighting.' },
];

const schema = [
  serviceSchema({
    name: 'Ceramic Coating',
    description: 'Professional mobile ceramic coating in Oakland and the Bay Area. Complete application includes decontamination, paint correction and a hydrophobic ceramic coating bonded to the paint, applied at your home or workplace.',
    path,
    image: '/images/ceramic-coating-oakland.jpg',
    offers: CERAMIC.map((c) => ({ name: `${c.name} Ceramic Package`, description: `Hand wash, iron and clay decontamination, ${c.prep.toLowerCase()}, professional ceramic coating, cure and inspection. Quoted per vehicle.` })),
  }),
  faqSchema(faqs),
  breadcrumbSchema([{ label: 'Services', href: '/services/' }, { label: 'Ceramic Coating', href: path }]),
];

const steps = [
  { title: 'Deep clean & decontamination', text: 'Detailed hand wash, iron remover and clay bar across every exterior surface. Oakland cars collect brake dust from 880 traffic and fallout near the Port, all of it has to come off first.' },
  { title: 'Paint correction & surface prep', text: 'Machine polishing removes swirls and scratches so the coating locks in a flawless finish, not old defects. Then a panel-wipe removes every trace of oil so the coating can bond.' },
  { title: 'Ceramic coating application', text: 'The coating is applied panel by panel, leveled and checked under light. Paint, and optionally trim, wheels and glass.' },
  { title: 'Cure & final inspection', text: 'The coating flashes and bonds; we inspect every panel for high spots before handing the vehicle back with care instructions. Full cure over the next 24 hours; no washing for 7 days.' },
];
---

<Base {title} {description} canonical={path} {schema} ogImage="/images/ceramic-coating-oakland.jpg" preloadImage="/images/ceramic-coating-oakland.jpg">
  <PageHero
    crumbs={[{ label: 'Services', href: '/services/' }, { label: 'Ceramic Coating' }]}
    eyebrow="Professional ceramic coating"
    showBook={false}
    titleHtml="Ceramic Coating in Oakland, <em>applied in your driveway</em>"
    text="A hard, glossy layer bonded to freshly corrected paint, so the car stays cleaner, washes in half the time and keeps its color. Every package includes the decontamination and polishing that make a coating worth having."
    image="/images/ceramic-coating-oakland.jpg"
    imageAlt="Ceramic coating being poured onto an applicator pad before a mobile application in Oakland, CA"
    facts={[
      { label: 'Packages', value: 'Bronze, Silver, Gold' },
      { label: 'Prep', value: 'Paint correction included' },
      { label: 'Price', value: 'Quoted for your vehicle' },
      { label: 'Time', value: 'A full day' },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container two-col">
      <div class="prose">
        <span class="eyebrow">What you are paying for</span>
        <h2>Most of a coating job happens before the coating</h2>
        <p>A ceramic coating locks in whatever is underneath it. Put it over swirled, contaminated paint and you seal the swirls in for years. That is why the coating itself is the last hour of a full day, and the hours before it go into washing, dissolving iron, claying, polishing out defects and wiping every panel clean of oils so the coating can bond.</p>
        <p>Once it cures, the coating takes the abuse your clear coat used to take. Sun works on the coating instead of fading the paint. Bird droppings, sap and bug splatter sit on top instead of etching in. Brake dust and road film cannot grip, and water beads and runs off instead of drying into spots.</p>
        <p>The everyday result is simple: a car that looks freshly detailed after a rinse.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-frame"><img src="/images/paint-correction-oakland.jpg" srcset="/images/paint-correction-oakland-400w.webp 400w, /images/paint-correction-oakland-680w.webp 680w, /images/paint-correction-oakland.jpg 1080w" sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 560px" alt="Swirl marks showing under an inspection light before paint correction and coating" width="1080" height="1080" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--soft" id="pricing">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Packages</span>
        <h2>Three packages. The difference is the prep.</h2>
        <p class="lead">Same professional coating in all three. What changes is how much paint correction goes underneath, which depends on the condition of your paint. Every coating is quoted for your vehicle, so you know the price before anything is booked.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cer">
        {CERAMIC.map((c) => (
          <article class:list={['cer__card', { 'cer__card--top': c.featured }]}>
            {c.featured && <span class="cer__flag">Most booked</span>}
            <h3>{c.name}</h3>
            <p class="cer__prep">{c.prep}</p>
            <p class="cer__for">{c.bestFor}</p>
            <ul class="checklist">
              <li>Hand wash, iron remover and clay bar</li>
              <li>{c.prep}</li>
              <li>Panel wipe so the coating bonds</li>
              <li>Professional ceramic coating on all paint</li>
              <li>Cure check and written care instructions</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="cer__cta">
              <a href={CTA.href} class="btn btn--primary btn--block" data-scroll-book>{CTA.label}</a>
            </div>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
      <h3 class="cer-add__head">Add to any package, quoted with your coating</h3>
      <div class="cer-add">
        {CERAMIC_ADDONS.map((a) => (
          <div><strong>{a.name}</strong><p>{a.desc}</p></div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Process steps={steps} heading="How we apply a coating" eyebrow="Our process" dark />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container two-col two-col--top">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Honest expectations</span>
        <h2>What a coating does, and what it cannot do</h2>
        <p class="lead">Coatings get oversold. Here is the straight version.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="honest">
        <div>
          <h3>It will</h3>
          <ul class="checklist">
            <li>Keep the paint glossy and the color from fading</li>
            <li>Stop sap, droppings and bugs etching in as fast</li>
            <li>Make the car much faster to wash</li>
            <li>Resist the fine marks normal washing leaves</li>
            <li>Keep brake dust from baking onto coated wheels</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>It will not</h3>
          <ul class="xlist">
            <li>Stop rock chips or key scratches. That is paint protection film.</li>
            <li>Survive brush car washes for long</li>
            <li>Fix failing or peeling clear coat</li>
            <li>Mean you never wash the car again</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--soft">
    <div class="container prose max-prose" style="margin-inline:auto">
      <span class="eyebrow">Bay Area cars</span>
      <h2>Why coatings make sense here</h2>
      <p>Bay Area paint deals with salt from the marine layer, iron-heavy brake dust from the freeways, sap and pollen from street trees, and strong sun inland. A sealant handles that for a few months. A coating handles it for years, and it turns a sap-covered hood in Piedmont or a salt-filmed car in Alameda into a five-minute rinse.</p>
      <p>Coatings pay off most on new cars, dark colors, cars that park outside and anyone who dislikes washing. New paint has the fewest defects to correct, and coating it before its first car wash means it never picks up the swirls most cars carry within a year.</p>

      <h2>Keeping a coating working</h2>
      <p>A coating needs gentle, regular washing to keep beading. Wash every two to three weeks with a pH-neutral soap, dry with microfiber or air, and skip brush car washes. Touchless washes are fine. When beading looks weak, it is usually road film on top of the coating, not the coating wearing out, and a decontamination wash brings it back.</p>
      <p>The easiest way to do all of that is a <a href="/membership/">membership</a>: we wash it the right way on a schedule and keep an eye on how the coating is holding up.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Reviews limit={3} filterService="Ceramic" heading="What people say about their coatings" />
  <Faq faqs={faqs} heading="Ceramic coating questions" />
  <QuoteSection eyebrow="Ceramic quote" title="Send us a photo of the paint in direct sun." text="Tell us the car and how it is parked. From a photo we can usually tell which package your paint needs, and we confirm the price and the day." />
  <ServicesGrid exclude={path} heading="Other services we bring to you" eyebrow="More from J&W" lead="" />
  <CtaBand title="Years of gloss, applied at your home." />
</Base>

<style>
  .cer { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 1.1rem; max-width: 1080px; margin: 0 auto; }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .cer { grid-template-columns: 1fr; max-width: 480px; } }
  .cer__card { position: relative; background: #fff; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 22px; padding: 1.6rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .cer__card--top { border: 2px solid var(--ink); box-shadow: var(--shadow); }
  .cer__flag { position: absolute; top: -0.75rem; left: 1.4rem; background: var(--red); color: #fff; font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 700; padding: 0.28rem 0.7rem; border-radius: 999px; }
  .cer__card h3 { font-size: 1.5rem; margin: 0; }
  .cer__prep { color: var(--red-text); font-weight: 650; font-size: 0.92rem; margin: 0.2rem 0 0.9rem; }
  .cer__for { color: var(--ink); font-size: 0.98rem; font-weight: 550; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .cer__card .checklist { font-size: 0.93rem; margin-bottom: 1.3rem; }
  .cer__cta { margin-top: auto; display: grid; gap: 0.5rem; }
  .cer-add__head { max-width: 1080px; margin: 2rem auto 0.9rem; font-size: 1.1rem; }
  .cer-add { max-width: 1080px; margin: 0 auto; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 1rem; }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .cer-add { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  .cer-add > div { border: 1px solid var(--line); background: #fff; border-radius: 16px; padding: 1rem 1.2rem; }
  .cer-add strong { color: var(--ink); }
  .cer-add p { margin: 0.3rem 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .honest { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1.25rem; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .honest { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  .honest > div { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 20px; padding: 1.4rem; }
  .honest h3 { font-size: 1.1rem; margin-bottom: 0.9rem; }
</style>
